@id     02_conditions
@title  Conditions
@topic  if/else
@lang   c

@theory
scanf reads input. &n means "the address of n", so scanf can store the value there.

    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    if (n > 10) {
        printf("big\n");
    } else {
        printf("small\n");
    }

You can chain checks with else if.

Your task: read one integer and print
positive  if it is greater than 0
negative  if it is less than 0
zero      if it is 0

@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);

    // your code here

    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    if (n > 0) printf("positive\n");
    else if (n < 0) printf("negative\n");
    else printf("zero\n");
    return 0;
}

@test
@input
5
@expected
positive

@test
@input
-3
@expected
negative

@hidden_test
@input
0
@expected
zero
